  "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/a9a098dc3e86546f67724803e1aeb1e092354afe27c78a0816274cc8fc877d78.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $6$ units, so the distance equals $6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/9781224c7cd05d4fe4456833bfdfd47ee914a3de7d8b57325e432e0e25aa1100.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then adding.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/6ac8084f27b1595a4f650d13a7ea4f8e11b193bb3e926fe855075fb0d0e49748.png)\n\nThe point $(-5,0)$ is at a distance of $5$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $(1,0)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $5+1=6.$",
